 Chapter 24 Cholesky Factors in Mental Health Research: ACE Decomposition and Longitudinal Covariance Models The previous two articles established the mathematical machinery of Cholesky decomposition and applied it to correlated random effects in multi-site clinical trials and to joint propensity modeling in psychiatric polypharmacy. Both problems shared a common motivation: multivariate dependence that is scientifically meaningful rather than a statistical nuisance. This article continues in that spirit, turning to two further problems where the Cholesky parameterization is not merely convenient but arguably necessary. The first concerns behavioral genetics — specifically, how to decompose the sources of covariance between two psychiatric phenotypes into genetic and environmental contributions. The second concerns longitudinal psychiatry — how to model the full covariance structure of repeated symptom assessments in a way that separates within-person stability from occasion-specific fluctuation. In both cases, the Cholesky lower triangular structure is not imposed arbitrarily; it encodes a substantive scientific ordering that is part of the model’s meaning. 24.1 Part I: The ACE Cholesky Decomposition in Behavioral Genetics 24.1.1 The Scientific Problem A central question in psychiatric epidemiology is whether two disorders share genetic origins or arise from largely independent etiological pathways. Major depressive disorder and generalized anxiety disorder, for example, co-occur at rates far exceeding what chance would predict, yet it is not obvious from co-occurrence alone whether this reflects shared genetic liability, shared environmental exposure, or both. The classical tool for addressing this question is the twin study, which contrasts phenotypic similarity in monozygotic (MZ) pairs — who share essentially all segregating genetic variants — with dizygotic (DZ) pairs, who share on average half. By decomposing phenotypic variance into additive genetic (A), shared environmental (C), and unique environmental (E) components, twin models allow direct estimation of how much of the covariance between two traits flows through each pathway. For a single trait, the ACE model is straightforward. The phenotypic variance is written as \(\sigma^2_P = \sigma^2_A + \sigma^2_C + \sigma^2_E\), and the within-pair covariance is \(	ext{Cov}_{MZ} = \sigma^2_A + \sigma^2_C\) for MZ twins and \(	ext{Cov}_{DZ} =  rac{1}{2}\sigma^2_A + \sigma^2_C\) for DZ twins. The difference between these two quantities identifies the genetic component. Extending this to two correlated traits introduces cross-trait, cross-twin covariances — e.g., the covariance between twin 1’s depression score and twin 2’s anxiety score — which carry the necessary information to estimate genetic and environmental contributions to the phenotypic correlation between the two traits. The Cholesky ACE decomposition for two traits parameterizes each component matrix — \(\mathbf{A}\), \(\mathbf{C}\), and \(\mathbf{E}\) — as a \(2 	imes 2\) lower triangular matrix, so that the genetic covariance matrix, for instance, is \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_A = \mathbf{L}_A \mathbf{L}_A^T\). This is not purely a computational convenience. The lower triangular structure imposes an implicit ordering: the first trait’s genetic factor loads on both traits, while only the second trait has a residual genetic factor after accounting for what it shares with the first. In effect, column 1 of \(\mathbf{L}_A\) represents a common genetic factor driving both phenotypes, and column 2 of \(\mathbf{L}_A\) captures genetic variance specific to the second phenotype. Whether this specificity is large or small is an empirical question, and its answer bears directly on whether the two disorders have distinct or shared genetic architectures. 24.1.2 Mathematical Structure For twin pair \(j\), let \(\mathbf{y}_j = (y_{j1,1}, y_{j1,2}, y_{j2,1}, y_{j2,2})^T\) denote the stacked outcomes — the two phenotypes for each of the two twins. Under the ACE model, each individual’s phenotype vector is \[ \mathbf{y}_{ji} =  oldsymbol{\mu} + \mathbf{a}_{ji} + \mathbf{c}_j + \mathbf{e}_{ji} \] where \(\mathbf{a}_{ji} \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{\Sigma}_A)\), \(\mathbf{c}_j \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{\Sigma}_C)\), and \(\mathbf{e}_{ji} \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{\Sigma}_E)\) are the additive genetic, shared environmental, and unique environmental contributions respectively. Shared environment is by definition the same for both twins in a pair — hence the subscript \(j\) rather than \(ji\) — while genetic and unique environmental terms are twin-specific, though the genetic terms for DZ pairs carry the additional constraint of \( rac{1}{2}\) correlation. The key consequence is that the expected within-pair phenotypic covariance matrices differ by zygosity. For MZ pairs, where \(	ext{Cor}(\mathbf{a}_{j1}, \mathbf{a}_{j2}) = \mathbf{I}\): \[ \mathbf{\Sigma}_{MZ} =  egin{bmatrix} \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C + \mathbf{\Sigma}_E &amp; \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C \ \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C &amp; \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C + \mathbf{\Sigma}_E \end{bmatrix} \] For DZ pairs, where \(	ext{Cor}(\mathbf{a}_{j1}, \mathbf{a}_{j2}) =  rac{1}{2}\mathbf{I}\): \[ \mathbf{\Sigma}_{DZ} =  egin{bmatrix} \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C + \mathbf{\Sigma}_E &amp;  rac{1}{2}\mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C \  rac{1}{2}\mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C &amp; \mathbf{\Sigma}_A + \mathbf{\Sigma}_C + \mathbf{\Sigma}_E \end{bmatrix} \] Each of the three component covariance matrices is parameterized using a Cholesky factor: \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_A = \mathbf{L}_A \mathbf{L}_A^T\), \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_C = \mathbf{L}_C \mathbf{L}_C^T\), and \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_E = \mathbf{L}_E \mathbf{L}_E^T\). Stan’s cholesky_factor_cov[^2] type (not cholesky_factor_corr, since the diagonal need not be 1) is the appropriate declaration for each, with weakly informative half-normal priors on the diagonal elements and normal priors on the off-diagonal. The genetic correlation between the two phenotypes — the quantity most directly bearing on shared etiology — is then recovered as \[ r_A =  rac{(\mathbf{L}_A)_{21}}{\sqrt{(\mathbf{L}_A)_{11}^2 + (\mathbf{L}_A)_{21}^2}} \] which is simply the cosine of the angle between the two rows of \(\mathbf{L}_A\). 24.1.3 Stan Model data { int&lt;lower=1&gt; N_MZ; // Number of MZ twin pairs int&lt;lower=1&gt; N_DZ; // Number of DZ twin pairs int&lt;lower=1&gt; P; // Number of phenotypes (P = 2) matrix[N_MZ, 2*P] Y_MZ; // MZ twin data: [twin1_pheno1, twin1_pheno2, twin2_pheno1, twin2_pheno2] matrix[N_DZ, 2*P] Y_DZ; // DZ twin data } parameters { vector[P] mu; // Phenotype means cholesky_factor_cov[P] L_A; // Cholesky of additive genetic covariance cholesky_factor_cov[P] L_C; // Cholesky of shared environmental covariance cholesky_factor_cov[P] L_E; // Cholesky of unique environmental covariance } transformed parameters { // Component covariance matrices cov_matrix[P] Sigma_A = L_A * L_A&#39;; cov_matrix[P] Sigma_C = L_C * L_C&#39;; cov_matrix[P] Sigma_E = L_E * L_E&#39;; // Total within-person phenotypic covariance cov_matrix[P] Sigma_P = Sigma_A + Sigma_C + Sigma_E; // 4x4 within-pair covariance matrices (block structure) matrix[2*P, 2*P] Sigma_pair_MZ; matrix[2*P, 2*P] Sigma_pair_DZ; // Diagonal blocks: total phenotypic covariance Sigma_pair_MZ[1:P, 1:P] = Sigma_P; Sigma_pair_MZ[(P+1):(2*P), (P+1):(2*P)] = Sigma_P; // Off-diagonal blocks: cross-twin covariance Sigma_pair_MZ[1:P, (P+1):(2*P)] = Sigma_A + Sigma_C; // MZ: full A + C Sigma_pair_MZ[(P+1):(2*P), 1:P] = Sigma_A + Sigma_C; Sigma_pair_DZ[1:P, 1:P] = Sigma_P; Sigma_pair_DZ[(P+1):(2*P), (P+1):(2*P)] = Sigma_P; Sigma_pair_DZ[1:P, (P+1):(2*P)] = 0.5 * Sigma_A + Sigma_C; // DZ: 0.5 A + C Sigma_pair_DZ[(P+1):(2*P), 1:P] = 0.5 * Sigma_A + Sigma_C; } model { // Priors on Cholesky diagonal elements (variance components &gt; 0) to_vector(diagonal(L_A)) ~ normal(0, 5); to_vector(diagonal(L_C)) ~ normal(0, 5); to_vector(diagonal(L_E)) ~ normal(0, 5); // Off-diagonal elements can take any sign L_A[2, 1] ~ normal(0, 2); L_C[2, 1] ~ normal(0, 2); L_E[2, 1] ~ normal(0, 2); mu ~ normal(0, 20); // Likelihoods for (n in 1:N_MZ) Y_MZ[n] ~ multi_normal(rep_vector(0.0, 2*P) + append_row(mu, mu), Sigma_pair_MZ); for (n in 1:N_DZ) Y_DZ[n] ~ multi_normal(rep_vector(0.0, 2*P) + append_row(mu, mu), Sigma_pair_DZ); } generated quantities { // Heritability for each phenotype real h2_1 = Sigma_A[1, 1] / Sigma_P[1, 1]; real h2_2 = Sigma_A[2, 2] / Sigma_P[2, 2]; // Genetic correlation between the two phenotypes real r_A = Sigma_A[1, 2] / sqrt(Sigma_A[1, 1] * Sigma_A[2, 2]); real r_E = Sigma_E[1, 2] / sqrt(Sigma_E[1, 1] * Sigma_E[2, 2]); // Phenotypic correlation and its genetic/environmental decomposition real r_P = Sigma_P[1, 2] / sqrt(Sigma_P[1, 1] * Sigma_P[2, 2]); } 24.1.4 R Implementation and Posterior Analysis library(rstan) library(MASS) library(ggplot2) library(dplyr) library(tidyr) # -------------------------------------------------- # Simulate bivariate twin data: depression + anxiety # -------------------------------------------------- set.seed(42) N_MZ &lt;- 300 N_DZ &lt;- 300 P &lt;- 2 # depression, anxiety # True genetic structure # Strong common genetic factor + some anxiety-specific genetic variance L_A_true &lt;- matrix(c(3.0, 0, 2.0, 1.5), nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE) L_C_true &lt;- matrix(c(1.5, 0, 1.2, 0.8), nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE) L_E_true &lt;- matrix(c(2.0, 0, 0.5, 2.5), nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE) Sigma_A_true &lt;- L_A_true %*% t(L_A_true) Sigma_C_true &lt;- L_C_true %*% t(L_C_true) Sigma_E_true &lt;- L_E_true %*% t(L_E_true) Sigma_P_true &lt;- Sigma_A_true + Sigma_C_true + Sigma_E_true # True genetic correlation (the key quantity) r_A_true &lt;- Sigma_A_true[1,2] / sqrt(Sigma_A_true[1,1] * Sigma_A_true[2,2]) r_E_true &lt;- Sigma_E_true[1,2] / sqrt(Sigma_E_true[1,1] * Sigma_E_true[2,2]) cat(&quot;True genetic correlation (r_A):&quot;, round(r_A_t