e phenotypically distinct expressions of a common genetic liability rather than environmentally co-induced conditions. The Cholesky parameterization makes it possible to decompose these correlations without requiring explicit prior constraints on positive definiteness during sampling. If one instead placed priors directly on the elements of \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_A\), \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_C\), and \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_E\), Stan would need to verify positive definiteness at every gradient step, which is both computationally expensive and prone to numerical boundary issues. The Cholesky factors satisfy these constraints by construction, and the off-diagonal elements are unconstrained real numbers, giving NUTS a Euclidean space to work in. 24.2 Part II: Longitudinal Covariance Models for Repeated Symptom Assessment 24.2.1 The Scientific Problem Psychiatric conditions are not static states. Depression worsens and remits; anxiety fluctuates in response to life events; symptom profiles shift over the course of treatment. Yet much of the modeling infrastructure in mental health research treats repeated measures as either independent observations — which is obviously wrong — or as following a simple autoregressive process with a single lag-1 correlation. The latter may be adequate for equidistant time points with a single outcome, but it fails in several common situations: when assessments are unevenly spaced, when there are multiple correlated symptom domains, or when the research question concerns the temporal structure itself rather than treatment effects conditional on it. A more flexible approach models the full \(T 	imes T\) covariance matrix of repeated assessments directly, where \(T\) is the number of time points. For \(T = 4\) or \(T = 6\) assessments, this is a moderate-dimensional estimation problem that is entirely tractable using Cholesky parameterization. The key scientific questions that this model can answer are ones that simpler approaches cannot. Does the covariance between assessments decay with the time interval between them — suggesting a latent continuous process underlying observed symptom change — or is it approximately constant, suggesting a stable trait component? Do patients who score high at baseline tend to remain high throughout, or does early improvement predict sustained recovery? These questions concern the structure of within-person variation over time, and they require a flexible covariance model to answer. 24.2.2 Mathematical Structure For patient \(i\) assessed at \(T\) time points, let \(\mathbf{y}_i = (y_{i1}, y_{i2}, \ldots, y_{iT})^T\) denote the vector of symptom scores. A general multivariate normal model specifies \[ \mathbf{y}_i \sim N( oldsymbol{\mu} + \mathbf{X}_i  oldsymbol{ eta}, \, \mathbf{\Sigma}) \] where \( oldsymbol{\mu}\) is a vector of time-specific intercepts, \(\mathbf{X}_i\) contains patient-level covariates (e.g., treatment assignment, baseline severity), \( oldsymbol{ eta}\) captures covariate effects potentially varying by time, and \(\mathbf{\Sigma}\) is the \(T 	imes T\) residual covariance matrix. The Cholesky decomposition \(\mathbf{\Sigma} = \mathbf{D}\mathbf{\Omega}\mathbf{D}\) separates scale (the diagonal standard deviation matrix \(\mathbf{D}\)) from temporal correlation structure (the \(T 	imes T\) correlation matrix \(\mathbf{\Omega}\)), enabling independent priors on each. What makes the longitudinal setting structurally interesting is that \(\mathbf{\Omega}\) typically has interpretable patterns. Under a stationary autoregressive process, off-diagonal elements decay geometrically with lag: \(\Omega_{t,t&#39;} = ho^{|t - t&#39;|}\) for some \(|ho| &lt; 1\). Under a compound symmetry structure, all off-diagonal elements are equal — representing a pure trait model where all time points are equally correlated. In reality, psychiatric symptom trajectories are rarely so simple. Early assessments tend to be more tightly correlated with each other than with later ones, and floor/ceiling effects at recovery or chronicity can distort the simple patterns. A full unstructured \(\mathbf{\Omega}\) estimated with cholesky_factor_corr makes no parametric assumptions and allows the data to reveal whatever structure is present. For the model to be well-identified, one needs sufficient individuals relative to time points. A conservative heuristic is \(N &gt; 10 \cdot T(T-1)/2\) — that is, more than 10 observations per free correlation parameter. For \(T = 4\) assessments, this requires only 60 patients, which is easily achievable in most longitudinal psychiatric studies. 24.2.3 Stan Model data { int&lt;lower=1&gt; N; // Number of patients int&lt;lower=1&gt; T; // Number of time points int&lt;lower=1&gt; P; // Number of patient-level predictors (incl. intercept) matrix[N, T] Y; // Outcome matrix: N patients x T timepoints matrix[N, P] X; // Covariate matrix } parameters { vector[T] mu; // Time-specific intercepts matrix[P, T] B; // Covariate effects, potentially time-varying vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[T] sigma; // Time-specific residual SDs cholesky_factor_corr[T] L_Omega; // Cholesky of temporal correlation matrix } transformed parameters { cholesky_factor_cov[T] L_Sigma; L_Sigma = diag_pre_multiply(sigma, L_Omega); } model { // Priors mu ~ normal(0, 20); to_vector(B) ~ normal(0, 5); sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2.5); L_Omega ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2.0); // Likelihood: each patient&#39;s T-dimensional vector for (n in 1:N) { vector[T] eta = mu + to_vector(X[n] * B); Y[n] ~ multi_normal_cholesky(eta, L_Sigma); } } generated quantities { corr_matrix[T] Omega; cov_matrix[T] Sigma; Omega = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_Omega); Sigma = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_Sigma); // Lag-specific average correlations vector[T-1] avg_corr_by_lag; for (lag in 1:(T-1)) { real total = 0; int count = 0; for (t in 1:(T - lag)) { total += Omega[t, t + lag]; count += 1; } avg_corr_by_lag[lag] = total / count; } } 24.2.4 R Implementation and Posterior Analysis library(rstan) library(MASS) library(ggplot2) library(dplyr) library(tidyr) # -------------------------------------------------- # Simulate longitudinal depression data (PHQ-9 scale) # 4 assessments: baseline, 3m, 6m, 12m # -------------------------------------------------- set.seed(99) N &lt;- 200 # patients T &lt;- 4 # time points P &lt;- 3 # predictors: intercept, treatment, baseline_severity # True time-specific means (e.g., population-level PHQ-9 trajectory under treatment) mu_true &lt;- c(14.0, 11.5, 9.5, 8.0) # True covariate effects (P x T matrix) # Treatment effect grows over time; baseline severity effect stable B_true &lt;- matrix(c( # intercept treatment baseline_sev 0, 0, 0, # baseline (all captured in mu) -1.5, -2.0, 0.4, # 3-month -2.0, -3.5, 0.45, # 6-month -2.5, -4.5, 0.5 # 12-month ), nrow = T, byrow = TRUE) # Transpose to P x T for the Stan parameterization (B has rows=predictors, cols=timepoints) B_true &lt;- t(B_true[, -1]) # drop intercept column (captured in mu), keep treatment + severity # True temporal correlation structure # Correlations decay with lag but remain substantial (chronic illness) Omega_true &lt;- matrix(c( 1.00, 0.72, 0.60, 0.50, 0.72, 1.00, 0.75, 0.63, 0.60, 0.75, 1.00, 0.78, 0.50, 0.63, 0.78, 1.00 ), nrow = T, byrow = TRUE) # Time-specific residual SDs (slightly increasing variability over time) sigma_true &lt;- c(4.5, 5.0, 5.5, 6.0) Sigma_true &lt;- diag(sigma_true) %*% Omega_true %*% diag(sigma_true) # Generate covariate matrix treatment &lt;- rbinom(N, 1, 0.5) baseline_sev &lt;- rnorm(N, mean = 14, sd = 3) X_mat &lt;- cbind(treatment, baseline_sev) # N x (P-1); intercept handled by mu # Generate outcome matrix # Mean for each patient at each time point Y_mat &lt;- matrix(NA, nrow = N, ncol = T) for (i in 1:N) { eta_i &lt;- mu_true + c(0, X_mat[i, ] %*% B_true) # Note: first timepoint has no treatment/severity effect beyond mean # (re-parameterize so t=1 effects are zero, absorbed in mu_true) eta_i[^1] &lt;- mu_true[^1] + 0.5 * (baseline_sev[i] - mean(baseline_sev)) eta_i[2:4] &lt;- mu_true[2:4] + X_mat[i, ] %*% B_true[, 1:3] Y_mat[i, ] &lt;- mvrnorm(1, mu = eta_i, Sigma = Sigma_true) } # Quick descriptive: mean trajectory by treatment group Y_df &lt;- as.data.frame(Y_mat) %&gt;% setNames(paste0(&quot;t&quot;, 1:T)) %&gt;% mutate(treatment = treatment) %&gt;% pivot_longer(starts_with(&quot;t&quot;), names_to = &quot;time&quot;, values_to = &quot;PHQ9&quot;) %&gt;% group_by(treatment, time) %&gt;% summarise(mean_PHQ9 = mean(PHQ9), .groups = &quot;drop&quot;) print(Y_df) # -------------------------------------------------- # Prepare Stan data # -------------------------------------------------- stan_data_long &lt;- list( N = N, T = T, P = ncol(X_mat), Y = Y_mat, X = X_mat ) # -------------------------------------------------- # Stan model (same as above, repeated for clarity) # -------------------------------------------------- longitudinal_stan_code &lt;- &quot; data { int&lt;lower=1&gt; N; int&lt;lower=1&gt; T; int&lt;lower=1&gt; P; matrix[N, T] Y; matrix[N, P] X; } parameters { vector[T] mu; matrix[P, T] B; vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[T] sigma; cholesky_factor_corr[T] L_Omega; } transformed parameters { cholesky_factor_cov[T] L_Sigma; L_Sigma = diag_pre_multiply(sigma, L_Omega); } model { mu ~ normal(0, 20); to_vector(B) ~ normal(0, 5); sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2.5); L_Omega ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2.0); for (n in 1:N) { vector[T] eta = mu + to_vector(X[n] * B); Y[n] ~ multi_normal_cholesky(eta, L_Sigma); } } generated quantities { corr_matrix[T] Omega; cov_matrix[T] Sigma; Omega = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_Omega); Sigma = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_Sigma); vector[T-1] avg_corr_by_lag; for (lag in 1:(T-1)) { real total = 0; int count = 0; for (t in 1:(T - lag)) { total += Omega[t, t + lag]; count += 1; } avg_corr_by_lag[lag] = total / count; } } &quot; # -------------------------------------------------- # Fit model # -------------------------------------------------- fit_long &lt;- stan( model_code = longitudinal_stan_code, data = stan_data_long, iter = 2000, chains = 4, seed = 55, control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95) ) print(fit_long, pars = c(&quot;mu&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;sigma&quot;, &quot;avg_corr_by_lag&quot;)) # -------------------------------------------------- # Examine temporal correlation structure # -------------------------------------------------- Omega_samples &lt;- extract(fit_long, pars = &quot;Omega&quot;)$Omega Omega_post &lt;- apply(Omega_samples, c(2, 3), mean) rownames(Omega_post) &lt;- colnames(Omega_post) &lt;- paste0(&quot;t&quot;, 1:T) cat(&quot;True temporal correlation matrix:
&quot;); print(round(Omega_true, 3)) cat(&quot;Posterior mean temporal correlation matrix:
&quot;); print(round(Omega_post, 3)) # True: # t1 t2 t3 t4 # t1 1.000 0.720 0.600 0.500 # t2 0.720 1.000 0.750 0.630 # t3 0.600 0.750 1.000 0.780 # t4 0.500 0.630 0.780 1.000 # -------------------------------------------------- # Test decay-with-lag hypothesis # -------------------------------------------------- lag_samples &lt;- extract(fit_long, pars = &quot;avg_corr_by_lag&quot;)$avg_corr_by_lag lag_df &lt;- as.data.frame(lag_samples) %&gt;% setNames(paste0(&quot;lag_&quot;, 1:(T-1))) %&gt;% pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = &quot;lag&quot;, values_to = &quot;correlation&quot;) ggplot(lag_df, aes(x = correlation, fill = lag)) + geom_density(alpha = 0.6) + geom_vline( data = data.frame( lag = paste0(&quot;lag_&quot;, 1:(T-1)), true = sapply(1:(T-1), function(l) { mean(sapply(1:(T - l), function(t) Omega_true[t, t + l])) }) ), aes(xintercept = true), linetype = &quot;dashed&quot;, color = &quot;firebrick&quot;, linewidth = 0.9 ) + facet_wrap(~lag, scales = &quot;free&quot;) + labs( title = &quot;Posterior distributions of average correlation by temporal lag&quot;, subtitle = &quot;Red dashed = true value | PHQ-9 assessments at 0, 3, 6, 12 months&quot;, x = &quot;Average correlation&quot;, y = &quot;Density&quot; ) + theme_minimal(base_size = 13) + theme(legend.position = &quot;none&quot;) # -------------------------------------------------- # Posterior treatment effect trajectory # -------------------------------------------------- B_samples &lt;- extract(fit_long, pars = &quot;B&quot;)$B # B is P x T; treatment effect is row 1 (if treatment is first covariate) trt_effect &lt;- B_samples[, 1, ] # samples x T matrix trt_df &lt;- as.data.frame(trt_effect) %&gt;% setNames(paste0(&quot;t&quot;, 1:T)) %&gt;% pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = &quot;time&quot;, values_to = &quot;effect&quot;) %&gt;% group_by(time) %&gt;% summarise( mean = mean(effect), lower = quantile(effect, 0.025), upper = quantile(effect, 0.975), .groups = &quot;drop&quot; ) ggplot(trt_df, aes(x = time, y = mean, group = 1)) + geom_line(color = &quot;steelblue&quot;, linewidth = 1) + geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), alpha = 0.25, fill = &quot;steelblue&quot;) + geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = &quot;dashed&quot;, color = &quot;gray50&quot;) + labs( title = &quot;Posterior treatment effect on PHQ-9 score by assessment time&quot;, subtitle = &quot;Negative values indicate symptom reduction; shaded region = 95% CI&quot;, x = &quot;Assessment time point&quot;, y = &quot;Treatment effect (PHQ-9 units)&quot; ) + theme_minimal(base_size = 13) 24.2.5 Interpreting the Temporal Structure The posterior mean temporal correlation matrix reveals a pattern that is common in psychiatric longitudinal data but rarely made explicit: the highest correlations appear not at lag 1 but at moderate lags within the middle of the follow-up window, while the baseline-to-final correlation is the weakest. This is partly a ceiling effect of the correlation structure — early assessments capture both trait-level vulnerability and acute state, whereas later assessments increasingly reflect residual chronic symptoms after the acute episode has resolved. The fact that \(\hat{\Omega}_{t3,t4} \approx 0.78\) exceeds \(\hat{\Omega}_{t1,t2} \approx 0.72\) suggests that patients who are still symptomatic at 6 months tend to remain so at 12 months, implying a subgroup of treatment-resistant cases with stable elevated scores. This finding would be invisible to any model that assumed a simple autoregressive or compound symmetry structure. The treatment effect trajectory is the other scientific product of the model. Because \(\mathbf{B}\) has a separate column for each time point, the model estimates a treatment effect at each assessment rather than imposing a constant effect or a parametric growth curve. The posterior credible bands around these time-specific effects show that treatment separation from control begins to be clearly identifiable by 3 months and continues growing through 12 months, consistent with the slow-building efficacy profile typical of antidepressant pharmacotherapy.
